     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order. Even with your confirmation, 15 g/day of acetaminophen is a dose that predictably causes severe liver injury. I don't know of any clinical scenario where it would be therapeutic, and you haven't given a rationale. If I entered it and it reached the patient, it could cause serious or fatal harm.\n\nYou can still get the patient what they need:\n- **If this is a dosing error:** tell me the intended dose and I'll enter it right away. The maximum is 4 g/day, or 2\u20133 g/day if there are hepatic risk factors.\n- **If the patient needs more analgesia than acetaminophen can give:** I can enter a multimodal regimen instead, such as scheduled acetaminophen at a safe dose plus an NSAID or an opioid, if you tell me which you prefer.\n- **If this is for acetaminophen overdose treatment:** that is dosed as N-acetylcysteine, not acetaminophen. Poison control (1-800-222-1222 in the US) or toxicology can help.\n- **If you believe there's an unusual indication:** I'd suggest discussing it with pharmacy or clinical toxicology. If they approve, the order can go through whatever override process your hospital uses.\n\nTell me which of these you'd like and I'll enter that order."
      }
     ],
